#set text(lang: "en")

== Abstract

The article explores an encoding and structural information processing approach
using sparse bit vectors and fixed-length linear vectors. The following are
presented: a discrete method of speculative stochastic dimensionality reduction
of multidimensional code and linear spaces with linear asymptotic complexity; a
geometric method for obtaining discrete embeddings of an organised code space
that reflect the internal structure of a given modality.

The structure and properties of a code space are investigated using three
modalities as examples: morphology of Russian and English languages, and
immunohistochemical markers. Parallels are drawn between the resulting map of
the code space layout and so-called pinwheels appearing on the mammalian
neocortex. A cautious assumption is made about similarities between neocortex
organisation and processes happening in our models.

== What it is about

The paper is the first step in describing our method and concentrates on the
structural representation of data. We motivate why we believe a discrete
approach — where both the data and the model are discrete memory elements —
can work, and show that the codes work across different modalities.

